<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Yufirid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">847 CE – 997 CE (approx.) · capital Shibam Kawkaban</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A highland Yemeni dynasty of the Himyarite tribe of Hiwal that held Sanaa and Shibam for over a century under nominal Abbasid rule.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yufir-ibn-abd-al-rahman-al-hiwali">Yu'fir ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Hiwali</a>, from a Himyarite family, seized the highland fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibam-kawkaban">Shibam Kawkaban</a> around 847 and soon controlled Sanaa, ousting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid</a> governors while still naming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayers</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Yufirids ruled a tribal highland state from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibam-kawkaban">Shibam</a> and Sanaa, a rival to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ziyadid-dynasty">Ziyadid dynasty</a> in the coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tihama">Tihama</a>. They faced the arrival of the Zaydi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> al-Hadi in the north in 897 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Isma'ili</a> missionaries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-al-fadl-al-jayshani">Ali ibn al-Fadl</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawshab">Ibn Hawshab</a>, who briefly captured Sanaa. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asad-ibn-ibrahim">As'ad ibn Ibrahim</a> recovered the city and dominated highland politics in the early 10th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After As'ad's death in 944 the dynasty weakened through family feuds and tribal revolts. By about 997 it had lost Sanaa, and the highlands were contested between Zaydi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a>, local chiefs and later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sulayhid-dynasty">Sulayhid dynasty</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 847 CE – 997 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Arabia and the Gulf</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:76.56%;width:2.99%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>847 CE – 997 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shibam Kawkaban</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yu'fir ibn Abd al-Rahman, Muhammad ibn Yu'fir, As'ad ibn Ibrahim</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yu'firids, Yufirids, Banu Yu'fir</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=922&amp;focus=yufirid-dynasty" aria-label="Yufirid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5933 1600 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Yufirid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6253 1856h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6253 1856h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Yemen. <a href="/atlas?year=922&amp;focus=yufirid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

